      "math_academy_id": "q-118289",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/bb7d3c1f1cf1ed19c22bad9eefc9f8c4911d70ed74231c5d63ed88a6cd710a67.png)\n\nThe diagram shows the vectors $a$ and $b.$  Which of the following diagrams illustrates the vector subtraction $-a+b.$",
      "worked_solution": "We interpret  $-a+b$ as the addition\n\n$$\n(-a)+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $-a$ is the vector that has the same magnitude as $a$ but goes in the direction *opposite* of $a.$\n\nFinally, we use the triangle law to add the vectors $-a$ and $b:$ \n\n- We can start $-a$ from an arbitrary point on the plane.\n\n- Next, we draw the vector that is equal to $b$ and has its tail placed at the head of $-a.$\n\n- Now, according to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $-\\text{a}$ to the head of $\\text{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196443/assets/c21de8788e5808a48d725252c6308e817b756ca20cc6bbe0446fbfc124440c58.png)",
